1
00:00:00,270 --> 00:00:02,640
A good scam call doesn't sound like one.

2
00:00:02,680 --> 00:00:05,240
It sounds polite. Even helpful.

3
00:00:06,920 --> 00:00:10,040
In ScamSuno, every caller is fictional.

4
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:12,160
Not every caller is lying.

5
00:00:12,620 --> 00:00:14,680
You can speak or type your reply.

6
00:00:14,720 --> 00:00:17,820
This demo is typed and recorded offline.

7
00:00:22,720 --> 00:00:26,680
Nothing here is obviously wrong.
That's what makes it hard.

8
00:00:26,720 --> 00:00:29,930
So you check somewhere
the caller can't control.

9
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:33,490
No matching notice.

10
00:00:33,530 --> 00:00:37,020
Not proof of fraud,
but not enough to trust the call.

11
00:00:39,320 --> 00:00:42,510
End the call to see
what was really going on,

12
00:00:42,550 --> 00:00:44,790
and which of your choices mattered.

13
00:00:45,320 --> 00:00:48,380
Anything the call didn't test isn't scored.

14
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:52,110
Some calls are genuine. Some are scams.

15
00:00:52,150 --> 00:00:54,130
Some never become clear.

16
00:00:54,170 --> 00:00:59,460
ScamSuno. Practise on a call that doesn't matter,
before one that does.
